19:T6d3,## The problem: `new ConcreteX()` everywhere

Sprinkle `new` calls through your code and every one of them welds a class to a specific implementation:

```
class Dialog {
    void render() {
        Button b = new WindowsButton();   // ← hard-wired
        b.onClick();
    }
}
```

To support a **web** button you now have to *open and edit `Dialog`*, the very class whose job is layout, not button-picking. That violates the **Open/Closed Principle** (you edit existing code to extend it) and the **Dependency Inversion Principle** (high-level layout logic depends on a concrete low-level widget).

## The Factory Method

Have the base **creator** declare an *abstract* factory method that returns a **product interface**, and let each subclass **override** it to pick the concrete product:

```
abstract class Dialog {
    abstract Button createBtn();          // the factory method
    void render() {
        Button b = createBtn();           // works against the interface
        b.onClick();
    }
}
class WebDialog extends Dialog {
    Button createBtn() { return new WebButton(); }
}
```

`render()` is shared code that never names a concrete button, it asks `createBtn()` and trusts the `Button` interface. Adding a `MacDialog` is a **new subclass**, with zero edits to `Dialog` or `render()`.

## Not the same as a simple factory

A **simple/static factory** is just one method with a `switch` that returns a product, handy, but you still edit that switch for every new type. The **Factory Method** pattern pushes the choice into **subclasses via polymorphism**: extension is a new class, not a new `case`.

> Use it when a class can't anticipate which concrete product it must create, and you want subclasses to specify that choice.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-creational","activeLesson":"factory-method"}],["$","$L18","factory-method",{"lesson":{"id":"factory-method","patternId":"lld-creational","title":"Factory Method","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Core","statement":"Define a method for creating an object, but let SUBCLASSES decide which concrete class to instantiate, so the creator code never names a product directly.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Uber"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Hard-wired new","code":["class Dialog {","    void render() {","        Button b = new WindowsButton();   // hard-coded concrete","        b.onClick();","    }","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Dialog.render() does `new WindowsButton()` directly, the layout class names a concrete widget.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"dlg","name":"Dialog","methods":["+ render()"],"x":1,"y":0.4,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[],"shownClassIds":["dlg"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["dlg"]},"vars":[["creates","new WindowsButton() 💥"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Now support a web button: you must open and EDIT Dialog. The class is tightly coupled to one concrete product, breaking OCP and DIP.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["dlg"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["dlg"]},"vars":[["add web button","must edit Dialog"]]}]},{"label":"Let subclasses decide","code":["abstract class Dialog {","    abstract Button createBtn();      // factory method","    void render() { createBtn().onClick(); }","}","class WinDialog extends Dialog {","    Button createBtn(){ return new WinButton(); }","}","class WebDialog extends Dialog {","    Button createBtn(){ return new WebButton(); }","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"Dialog.render() calls createBtn(), but createBtn is ABSTRACT, so the base class doesn't know which button it makes. It just trusts the factory method.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"dlg","name":"Dialog","stereotype":"abstract","methods":["+ render()","# createBtn(): Button"],"x":0.2,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"btn","name":"Button","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ onClick()"],"x":2.2,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent2"},{"id":"win","name":"WinDialog","methods":["# createBtn()"],"x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"web","name":"WebDialog","methods":["# createBtn()"],"x":1.1,"y":1.6},{"id":"winb","name":"WinButton","x":1.9,"y":1.6},{"id":"webb","name":"WebButton","x":2.6,"y":1.6}],"relations":[{"id":"wi","from":"win","to":"dlg","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"we","from":"web","to":"dlg","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"wib","from":"winb","to":"btn","kind":"implement"},{"id":"web2","from":"webb","to":"btn","kind":"implement"},{"id":"cr","from":"dlg","to":"btn","kind":"depend","label":"creates"}],"shownClassIds":["dlg"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["dlg"]},"vars":[["createBtn()","abstract"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"Button is the product interface render() works against, it only ever calls onClick(), never a concrete type.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["dlg","btn"],"shownRelIds":["cr"],"activeIds":["btn"]},"vars":[["render() uses","Button (interface)"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"WinDialog and WebDialog extend Dialog and OVERRIDE createBtn() to return their own button. The subclass supplies the choice.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["dlg","btn","win","web"],"shownRelIds":["cr","wi","we"],"activeIds":["win","web"]},"vars":[["subclasses","pick the product"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":6,"narration":"The concrete WinButton and WebButton each implement Button, so render() can drive either through the same interface.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["dlg","btn","win","web","winb","webb"],"shownRelIds":["cr","wi","we","wib","web2"],"activeIds":["winb","webb"]},"vars":[["both implement","Button"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"Trace a WebDialog: the Client calls render(), shared base-class code that knows nothing about web buttons.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"c","label":"Client","kind":"actor"},{"id":"d","label":"WebDialog","tint":"accent"},{"id":"b","label":"WebButton"}],"messages":[{"id":"m1","from":"c","to":"d","label":"render()","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"d","to":"d","label":"createBtn()","kind":"self"},{"id":"m3","from":"d","to":"b","label":"WebButton","kind":"create"},{"id":"m4","from":"d","to":"c","label":"done","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0}},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"render() calls the factory method createBtn() on itself. Because this object is a WebDialog, the overridden version runs.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:4:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:4:sequence:messages","shownCount":2,"activeIndex":1},"vars":[["render()","calls createBtn()"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":9,"narration":"WebDialog.createBtn() instantiates a WebButton and hands it back as a Button. The subclass supplied the right product.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:4:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:4:sequence:messages","shownCount":3,"activeIndex":2},"vars":[["returns","a WebButton as Button"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"render() drives the product through the interface and finishes, it never named a concrete button. That decoupling is the Factory Method.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:4:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:4:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":3},"vars":[["render()","uses Button, not a concrete class ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
